  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe quiet weather pattern continues through the first half of \nthe week, though a decaying shortwave will bring some increase \nin cloud cover later today into tonight. Otherwise, today will \nremain dry with plenty of sunshine, while the additional clouds \ntonight should help keep temperatures from falling quite as much\nas recent nights. Lows will generally be in the 40s, with some \ncolder interior spots potentially dipping into the upper 30s. \nThe added cloud cover should also help limit the coverage of fog\ntonight into Monday morning. \n\nThe surface high gradually shifts away Monday into Tuesday, \nallowing return flow to develop and temperatures to warm. Expect\nmostly sunny skies and a continued warming trend, with highs \nreaching the 70s both days and some locations potentially \npushing into the upper 70s by Tuesday. Despite the warmer \nafternoons, the dry airmass will continue to support fairly cool\novernight temperatures, generally in the 40s. Overall, \nconditions look dry and quiet through Tuesday.\n\nA change in the pattern arrives Tuesday night into Wednesday as\na northern-stream wave moves through the Great Lakes and begins\nto pull deeper moisture farther north. Shower chances will \nincrease Tuesday night/ Wednesday (especially Wednesday night), \nbut there remains uncertainty in how much precipitation actually\nreaches northern Michigan with very dry air already in place. \nThe timing and eventual track of the wave will be important in \ndetermining how much of a push this system makes into the \nregion. With the wave currently still favored to pass during the\nnighttime and relatively dry air still in place, rainfall may \nbe limited, with more meaningful amounts likely dependent on any\nconvective enhancement. \n\nThrough the latter half of the week, the active precipitation \ncorridor to our south may gradually lift northward, keeping at \nleast low-end shower chances in the forecast, particularly \nacross southern portions of the area. There should still be \nplenty of dry time, and forecast trends could continue to shift \nwetter or drier depending on how far north the moisture and \nforcing associated with the southern system can get. \nTemperatures will trend cooler following the midweek wave, with \nhighs settling back into the 60s toward the end of the week and \novernight lows generally in the 40s or slightly cooler.",
